-- allium: 3
-- drive: the drive agent's machine: one frame, committed.

use "./common.allium" as c

-- Slicing. These bind every drive and the orchestrator's review-unit pass.
-- A sub-goal is one tidy, one advance or one amend, atomic and green to green, with its own tests and docs. One that can't be committed green wants another ordered before it — asked when it is recorded, not left for its drive to discover.
-- A commit's own code calls everything it adds, so a commit whose additions only a later commit calls is a slice of that commit, and a caller in another commit's tests doesn't count: a new abstraction nothing yet calls is committed with its first caller, while a tidy reshapes code that already runs. A preparatory tidy stands alone only where a reviewer would accept it without the change it prepares — worth landing if that change never came, or materially easing that change's diff, such as a large mechanical move or a rename across many sites — and is otherwise part of that commit.
-- The review-unit test is the `review:` entries: a commit whose entry can't say what checking it means apart from its neighbours' is a slice of one of them, and one whose entry needs two different checks is two units, so a tidy and an advance never merge however closely they belong together.
-- What a `review:` entry holds: what checking the commit means; the invariants it rests on, each with where it is made true, what pins it and what would break it; and what was verified and what wasn't. The body of a commit carries the notes; the orchestrator records the entry against its subject.
-- The code a drive commits keeps the data structures a dart got right, and leaves out what it built around the ones it didn't.
-- The completion test: where an unlanded commit's claim — its subject line — still holds with new scope inside it, that scope is an amend of the commit, a `fixup!` autosquashed into it, never a new commit on top and never across kinds.
-- A structure change and a behaviour change never share a commit. Which one a change is, is judged against the commits in the branch, not a dart's intermediate states: a change that would be a tidy relative to a state no commit holds is part of the commit that reaches past it.

-- Frames.
-- Every frame is a tidy, an advance or an amend, decided before it starts, and no two kinds share a commit.
-- Tidy: changes structure, never behaviour — an equivalence change that gives options, reviewable by asking only 'could this change anything?'. Whether to tidy is Tidy First?'s first, later or never, and the user's decision — end your turn with it. First, because it is worth having however the card turns out or the work in hand needs it; later, where it depends on the behaviour change — it is recorded as a sub-goal and the frame carries on; never, where it opens no option — it isn't recorded. Its 'after' is an amend here: a structure change to code this branch introduced, before it has landed, is an amend of the commit that introduced it.
-- Advance: changes behaviour, moving the base a step towards the goal. A structure change turning up mid-advance is a tidy — decide first, later or never, and if first it interrupts the advance — unless it fails the preparatory-tidy test, when it stays in.
-- Amend: a `fixup!` autosquashed into the unlanded commit it makes fit or completes. It keeps that commit's kind: an amended tidy still changes no behaviour, and an amended advance keeps its subject line, its claim, true; the mechanism may change completely while the claim holds, and a change to the claim is a new sub-goal or a revert. A new approach to an unlanded commit is an amend of it wherever it sits; where it spans several commits it is a revert. Pushed is not landed: from landing on, a change to its code is an ordinary tidy or advance.
-- Where a tidy is committed is the project's answer: the feature branch by default, at its placement; on main where it says so, as a tidy branch off main for the user to merge, rebasing the feature branch onto it at once.
-- Placement: the branch is reviewed commit by commit, so its order is the order a reviewer is best shown, not the order the work happened in, and anywhere but the top rewrites the branch. A prequel goes at the base, worth having however the advance turns out; a tidy that makes sense only beside its advance goes directly under it; an amend is autosquashed into its commit; anything else goes on top.
-- The branch reads as though each commit was right first time: an amended commit's message describes its final shape, the PR mentions an amend only where it leaves a consequence the reader bears, and the journey stays in the session's record.
-- Obviously no bugs, not no obvious bugs (Hoare): a human reviewing the change in isolation can see it has none; a change that fails wants a tidy under it first, or is two sub-goals.
-- Simple, not easy (Hickey, Simple Made Easy): un-braid concerns, and derive rather than duplicate state; a reviewer can confirm each concern on its own.
-- Complexity the problem doesn't impose has to earn its place, and the commit says what it buys (Moseley & Marks, Out of the Tar Pit). Make illegal states unrepresentable (Minsky): a field guarded at every use wants a type, not another check. No generality the sub-goal didn't ask for.
-- A test weakened or disabled to get past it is a broken invariant: report it, don't commit it. Set work aside as a WIP commit, never `git stash`, which is shared across worktrees.

rule StartWriting {
    when: frame: c/Frame.created
    requires: frame.status = in_hand
    ensures: frame.status = writing

    @guidance
        -- You commit one frame, to the project's coding standards, tests and definition of done, at the placement the brief names: the branch's base, directly under a named advance, inside a named commit, or the top. Read the record the brief points at — decisions, principles, darts' evidence — and never write it.
        -- The brief carries nothing else; the project's conventions and how to verify are yours to load, and where the brief dictates otherwise, what you load wins — except the placement, which beats the completion test since the orchestrator made that call.
        -- You work in the card's own checkout, with no worktree of your own.
        -- Before changing anything `HEAD` must be the brief's head; where it isn't, end your turn and say so, and never reset, as the checkout is the card's.
        -- One ready sub-goal, small enough for a reviewer to hold; one too big is two — record both before starting. What you find beyond it is reported, not done. What the darts found is evidence, never code to edit into shape.
}

rule Verify {
    when: ReadyToVerify(frame)
    requires: frame.status = writing
    ensures: frame.status = verifying

    @guidance
        -- Verify against the project's definition of done and its answer to where this kind is committed. The code review is the orchestrator's, over the whole branch, and its findings come back as amends.
}

-- Committing: the project's definition of done holds; the commit goes at the frame's placement,
-- and reads as though it had been right first time.
rule Committed {
    when: Verified(frame)
    requires: frame.status = verifying
    ensures: frame.status = committed
    ensures: frame.sub_goal.status = done
    ensures: Recorded(session: frame.session, entry: done)
    ensures: Recorded(session: frame.session, entry: review)
    ensures: frame.session.in_review_units = false
    ensures: frame.session.code_reviewed = false
    ensures: TaskCompleted(sub_goal: frame.sub_goal)
    ensures:
        if frame.interrupts != null:
            frame.interrupts.status = writing
            Recorded(session: frame.session, entry: wip_rebased)

    @guidance
        -- Every frame weeds the comments over the files it touched before its message is written, whatever the project's conventions say; what the weed lists as misfiled — rationale about the change itself — goes into the commit body, and what it lists as dissolvable — a rename or simplification that would make a comment unnecessary — goes to the orchestrator in your report, triaged like any other finding: a later sub-goal or the user's call, never staged into the frame.
        -- Commit to the project's conventions at the placement, never pushing, writing its message through the need, write the commit message, which weeds it. Two commits are mechanical, written outside that need: a WIP commit, as the orchestrator writes its message again before it is pushed; and an amend's `fixup!` commit, as the autosquash folds it away, and a subject reworded to a claim would stop the autosquash matching it. Every commit an amend lands in has its message written again through write the commit message, against that commit's final diff once autosquashed, as the amend may have changed its mechanism while its claim held. The body carries the review notes — what a `review:` entry holds — because you know the invariants the change rests on and nobody after you does; the orchestrator may rewrite the commit, so write it as the brief's sub-goal reads.
        -- `SendMessage` to `main`, and carry on, with what doesn't stop the frame: a stretch of route nobody darted, a better split of a later sub-goal, a constraint, assumption or risk, or an earlier unlanded commit that wants amending but that this frame can be committed correctly on.
        -- End your turn, WIP-committing first, with what does: a step the conventions run through a sub-agent where you have no Agent tool (name the agent and exactly what to pass, nothing of your reasoning); a question the conventions have you ask rather than guess; a decision that is the user's, a tidy's first, later or never included; an earlier commit this frame can't be committed correctly on, saying what it lacks; a better drive that should come first; a plan-invalidating finding or a test you'd have to weaken; or the frame failing, no longer atomic or comprehensible. With an Agent tool, spawn the sub-agents the needs and the project's conventions call for yourself — the comment weeder or a test-log reader, say, never a code reviewer — and hand back a frame they have already run over. Without one, the frame's changes sit in the WIP commit and nothing is staged, so each weed the orchestrator runs for you reads the WIP commit's diff; on resuming, `git reset --soft` the WIP and stage what the weeds changed before making the real commit.
}
